      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        Canada Immigration Levels Plan 2027–2029: What to Expect Before It Is Released
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-4">
        The next federal Levels Plan is due within weeks. Many of its 2027 numbers are already in the current plan, and one commitment, cutting temporary residents below 5% of the population by the end of 2027, is far from met. This guide covers what is known, what is not, and what BC PNP candidates should watch.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <time datetime="2026-10-14">October 14, 2026</time>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <span>11 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-indigo-600 mb-2">380,000</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Notional 2027 PR target (current plan)</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-green-600 mb-2">92,500</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Notional 2027 PNP target</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-red-600 mb-2">6.7%</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Temporary residents, share of population (July 1, 2026, prelim.)</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-2">This article is written before the plan's release</p>
        <p class="text-amber-800">As of October 14, 2026, the 2027–2029 Immigration Levels Plan has not been tabled. Figures below come from the 2026–2028 plan and other official sources. Anything about what the new plan "may" do is our analysis, clearly labelled. We will update this page once the plan is public.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What the Levels Plan is, and when it arrives</h2>

      <p>
        Every year the federal immigration minister must table an Annual Report to Parliament on Immigration. Under section 94 of the <em>Immigration and Refugee Protection Act</em>, the report must include the number of people projected to become permanent residents in the following year. In practice it contains a three-year plan. The first year is a firm target, and the next two are "notional" targets that are revisited a year later.
      </p>
      <p>
        The deadline is set in law: on or before <strong>November 1</strong>, or, if a House of Parliament is not sitting then, within the next 30 days on which that House sits. November 1, 2026 is a Sunday, so the exact tabling day depends on the House calendar. IRCC's own committee briefing from April 2026 says the 2027–2029 plan "will be tabled in fall 2026," and its consultation page says the same.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The 2027 numbers already on paper</h2>

      <p>
        The 2026–2028 plan already set notional 2027 targets. IRCC says these "will be confirmed or adjusted by November 1 of each year." That makes the 2027 column below the starting point for the new plan.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Category</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">2026 target</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">2027 notional (range)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr class="font-semibold bg-slate-50"><td class="px-4 py-3">Overall PR admissions</td><td class="px-4 py-3">380,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">380,000 (350,000–420,000)</td></tr>
            <tr class="font-semibold"><td class="px-4 py-3">Economic total</td><td class="px-4 py-3">239,800</td><td class="px-4 py-3">244,700 (229,000–268,000)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Federal High Skilled (Express Entry)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">109,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">111,000 (86,000–122,000)</td></tr>
            <tr class="bg-green-50"><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8"><strong>Provincial Nominee Program</strong></td><td class="px-4 py-3"><strong>91,500</strong></td><td class="px-4 py-3"><strong>92,500 (82,000–106,000)</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Federal economic pilots</td><td class="px-4 py-3">8,175</td><td class="px-4 py-3">8,775 (6,600–12,400)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Atlantic Immigration Program</td><td class="px-4 py-3">4,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">4,000 (3,000–5,000)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Federal Business</td><td class="px-4 py-3">500</td><td class="px-4 py-3">500 (250–1,000)</td></tr>
            <tr class="font-semibold"><td class="px-4 py-3">Family total</td><td class="px-4 py-3">84,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">81,000 (75,000–90,000)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Spouses, partners and children</td><td class="px-4 py-3">69,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">66,000 (60,000–71,000)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Parents and grandparents</td><td class="px-4 py-3">15,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">15,000 (13,000–19,000)</td></tr>
            <tr class="font-semibold"><td class="px-4 py-3">Refugees and protected persons</td><td class="px-4 py-3">49,300</td><td class="px-4 py-3">49,300 (42,000–55,000)</td></tr>
            <tr class="font-semibold"><td class="px-4 py-3">Humanitarian and other</td><td class="px-4 py-3">6,900</td><td class="px-4 py-3">5,000 (4,000–7,000)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Francophone admissions outside Quebec</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9% (30,267)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">9.5% (31,825)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Quebec's skilled worker and business numbers are shown as "TBD" because Quebec sets its own targets under the Canada–Quebec Accord. Two one-time initiatives sit outside these totals. About 115,000 protected persons already on a path to PR will be moved through over two years. And up to 33,000 work permit holders will get an accelerated path to PR in 2026 and 2027.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Temporary resident arrivals</h3>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">New arrivals</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">2026</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">2027 notional</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr class="font-semibold"><td class="px-4 py-3">Total temporary arrivals</td><td class="px-4 py-3">385,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">370,000 (360,000–380,000)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Workers</td><td class="px-4 py-3">230,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">220,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">International Mobility Program</td><td class="px-4 py-3">170,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">170,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 pl-8">Temporary Foreign Worker Program</td><td class="px-4 py-3">60,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">50,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Students</td><td class="px-4 py-3">155,000</td><td class="px-4 py-3">150,000</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The pressure point: the 5% temporary resident goal</h2>

      <p>
        The government has committed to bringing temporary residents below 5% of Canada's population by the end of 2027. Progress so far:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>IRCC told a parliamentary committee in April 2026 that the share had come down from a peak of 7.6% to 6.5% (as of January 1, 2026).</li>
        <li>Statistics Canada's preliminary second-quarter estimate, released September 23, 2026, put the number of non-permanent residents at 2,779,774 on July 1, 2026, out of a total population of 41,798,407. That works out to about 6.7%. Statistics Canada notes that preliminary figures are revised later.</li>
        <li>In British Columbia, the same release counted 465,514 non-permanent residents out of 5,710,598 people on July 1, 2026, about 8.2% by our calculation. That is higher than the national share.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Our analysis:</strong> going from about 6.7% to under 5% in roughly 18 months would mean well over 600,000 fewer temporary residents at today's population size. The 2027–2029 plan will show how the government means to close that gap. The main options are lower temporary arrival targets, more transitions from temporary to permanent status (which count against the PR target), or a later deadline. Each choice matters for people already in Canada on work permits. We cover this in our <a href="/articles/canada-temporary-resident-cap-2027-bc-work-permit-holders" class="text-blue-600 underline">temporary resident cap guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What Canadians and stakeholders were asked</h2>

      <p>
        IRCC's consultation for this plan ran from <strong>May 12 to June 30, 2026</strong>. This year's survey switched to open-ended questions. The published questions show the commitments the department treats as settled. Question 19 lists three:
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Reducing the temporary population below 5% of the total by the end of 2027</li>
        <li>Stabilizing permanent resident admissions below 1% of the population after 2027</li>
        <li>Raising the Francophone admissions target outside Quebec to 12% by 2029</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The other open questions asked about regional pressures and demographic trends (Q20), priorities beyond 2029 (Q21) and barriers to good outcomes (Q22). The consultation page lists priorities including refugee protection, family reunification, Francophone immigration outside Quebec, and matching immigration levels to housing, infrastructure and public services capacity, as well as filling labour gaps.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Why the "below 1%" line matters:</strong> 1% of a population of about 41.8 million is about 418,000. A PR target of 380,000 sits under that line. The commitment caps how far PR targets could rise after 2027. It does not require a cut.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Seven things to check on release day</h2>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-4">
        <li><strong>The overall 2027 PR target.</strong> Is it confirmed at 380,000, or changed? The 1% commitment caps any increase.</li>
        <li><strong>The PNP line for 2027 and the first figure for 2029.</strong> This is the number that matters most for provincial programs. A drop below 92,500 would squeeze provinces. A higher number gives room for more allocations.</li>
        <li><strong>Federal High Skilled.</strong> Express Entry admissions affect how much of the economic share goes to federal selection versus provinces. The notional 2027 figure is 111,000.</li>
        <li><strong>Temporary arrival targets.</strong> Watch whether 2027 is cut below 370,000 and how the worker split (International Mobility Program vs TFWP) changes.</li>
        <li><strong>New or extended one-time initiatives.</strong> The current 33,000 work permit holder measure covers 2026 and 2027. Any successor would show up here.</li>
        <li><strong>Francophone targets.</strong> 9.5% is set for 2027 and 10.5% for 2028, on the way to 12% by 2029. A firm 2029 figure would confirm the path. This affects French-speaking candidates in every province; see our <a href="/articles/canada-pr-planning-guide-french-advantage-2026" class="text-blue-600 underline">French advantage guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Supplementary notes on PNP allocations.</strong> The plan sets the national total. The provincial split comes later, when IRCC notifies each province of its allocation.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">From national target to BC's allocation</h2>

      <p>
        A national PNP number does not become BC nominations on the same day. IRCC first tables the plan, then notifies each province of its allocation, and may add mid-year increases. BC's last two cycles show how long that takes:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Date</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Event (per WelcomeBC)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Jan 31, 2025</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2025 allocation confirmed at 4,000, a 50% reduction from 2024</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Oct 2, 2025</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,254 additional nominations for 2025</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Dec 15, 2025</td><td class="px-4 py-3">960 more; final 2025 allocation 6,214</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Dec 18, 2025</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2026 allocation set at 5,254; BC had requested 9,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">Aug 18, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">1,000 additional; 2026 allocation 6,254, to be used by December 31, 2026</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        By our arithmetic, BC's current 6,254 is about 6.8% of the 2026 national PNP target of 91,500. <strong>Illustration only, not a forecast:</strong> if the 2027 national target stayed at 92,500 and BC's share stayed the same, that would point to an allocation of a similar size to 2026's final figure. In practice, BC's opening allocation has been well below its final allocation in both recent years. Allocations are negotiated, and BC has said it "continues to advocate" for more. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-2027-outlook-predictions" class="text-blue-600 underline">BC PNP 2027 outlook</a> looks at what different allocation outcomes would mean for draws.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The Express Entry piece</h2>

      <p>
        The Levels Plan sets <em>how many</em> people come through Express Entry. The categories decide <em>who</em> gets invited. IRCC ran a separate consultation from August 4 to September 1, 2026 on 2027 categories. It asked about expanding efforts to attract highly skilled workers, <em>narrowing</em> the number or eligibility of labour-shortage categories, and keeping the Francophone focus. IRCC's 2026–27 Departmental Plan also mentions developing Express Entry reforms "including the addition of points for job offers." None of this has been finalized. For BC PNP candidates using the <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-express-entry-bc-eebc-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 underline">Express Entry BC option</a>, the provincial nomination still matters most. Read our <a href="/articles/express-entry-2027-category-changes" class="text-blue-600 underline">Express Entry 2027 guide</a> for the category details.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Three plausible scenarios (analysis)</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Scenario</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">What it would look like</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">What it would mean for BC PNP candidates</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Status quo</td><td class="px-4 py-3">2027 notional targets confirmed; small tweaks to TR numbers</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Allocation likely similar in scale to 2026; Care/Build/Innovate draws continue at a similar pace</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Tighter</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Lower TR arrivals and/or a lower economic or PNP line to hit the 5% goal</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Fewer nominations and higher cutoffs; work-permit timing becomes urgent</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Transition-heavy</td><td class="px-4 py-3">PR total held, but more space reserved for people already in Canada</td><td class="px-4 py-3">In-Canada workers with B.C. job offers benefit; candidates abroad face more competition</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        We are not assigning probabilities. The public record supports the first and third scenarios more than the second, because IRCC has consistently described PR as "stabilizing" and has used one-time transition measures. But the temporary resident gap is real, and governments can change course.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What to do before the plan lands</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Do not wait for the plan to register.</strong> BC PNP registration is free and your place in the pool depends on your score, not the federal plan. Check your score with our <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP calculator</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Map your status expiry dates.</strong> If your work permit ends in 2027, plan around the slow start to BC PNP draws seen in both 2025 and 2026.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep language tests valid.</strong> BC PNP results are valid for two years and must be valid at registration to earn points.</li>
        <li><strong>Budget for fees.</strong> The BC PNP application fee is $1,750. See our <a href="/articles/canada-pr-application-fees-2027" class="text-blue-600 underline">PR fees 2027 guide</a> for federal costs.</li>
        <li><strong>Have a second route.</strong> If your occupation is not a BC priority, compare other provinces in our <a href="/articles/best-provinces-pnp-2027" class="text-blue-600 underline">best provinces for PNP 2027</a> guide.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
